WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:04.950
The Mandalorian's second season has really
put Din Djarin through the wringer.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:09.130
In addition to fighting a krayt dragon and
one horde after the next of enemies, he's

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:14.450
also had his entire belief system questioned
upon learning he's actually a Child of the

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:19.470
Watch, an extremist sect of Mandalorians who
aim to reestablish the ancient order of their

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:20.470
kind.

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:23.849
Part of their creed involves never removing
one's helmet, which Din has stuck to.

00:00:23.849 --> 00:00:28.220
There have only been two times so far in the
series when we've seen Din's face.

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:32.579
The first comes when he suffers injuries and
has to remove his helmet for treatment, only

00:00:32.579 --> 00:00:36.670
revealing his face to an unliving droid (voiced
by Taika Waititi.

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:41.539
The second time occurs in Chapter 15, when
Din has to gain access to sensitive materials

00:00:41.539 --> 00:00:43.750
from the Empire by showing his face.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:47.960
It appears as though Din is questioning his
belief system more and more in recent episodes,

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:52.679
and even though he's still hesitant to remove
his helmet, he's willing to do so if it means

00:00:52.679 --> 00:00:54.089
saving Grogu.

00:00:54.089 --> 00:00:57.829
Rumors have been circling that the reason
for these increasingly common face reveals

00:00:57.829 --> 00:01:02.000
lies in Pedro Pascal wanting more facetime
on the Star Wars series.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.600
Some rumors even extend to the point of saying
Pascal would leave the show if he continued

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.200
being hidden behind all that beskar all the
time.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:13.810
In a recent television appearance, he laid
those rumors to rest and assured fans that

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:16.729
he's perfectly happy doing what's best for
the show.

00:01:16.729 --> 00:01:21.670
Audiences will be able to see Pedro Pascal's
face front and center in the upcoming superhero

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:25.939
sequel Wonder Woman 1984 as the villainous
Maxwell Lord.

00:01:25.939 --> 00:01:30.979
As such, he's been doing a whole lot of media
appearances in promotion of the film.

00:01:30.979 --> 00:01:35.700
When he called in virtually on The One Show,
the topic shifted to his work on The Mandalorian.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:39.310
He was asked point-blank about the rumors
that purport he wants his helmet off more

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:40.310
often.

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:41.750
Pascal was quick to deny them.

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:44.090
After saying the rumors aren't true, he went
on to state,

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:46.649
"It's a really wonderful way of telling the
story.

00:01:46.649 --> 00:01:50.570
It's always been a very clear creed for the
character and the collaborative process of

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:53.479
the whole thing, we've all been on the same
page with this.

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:57.619
So what I want is for them to make the best
show possible, however they get that done."

00:01:57.619 --> 00:02:01.890
It sounds as though Pascal understands why
the creative forces behind the show want Din

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:06.280
Djarin's helmet on, and he's totally cool
with going along for the ride and performing

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:08.490
however is best for the character.

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:12.630
It's clear how deeply the character believes
in the Mandalorian creed, and when he takes

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:16.700
off his helmet in Chapter 15, it is obvious
how uncomfortable it makes him.

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:20.680
Perhaps he'll open up more in the future,
when more circumstances arise that require

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:22.030
him to reveal himself.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:26.080
By all accounts, Pascal will be portraying
Din Djarin for quite some time to come.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:30.290
Disney has announced The Mandalorian will
return for season three sometime in December

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:32.550
2021 (via Collider).

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:36.950
During the recent Disney Investor Day event,
the company announced a slew of Star Wars

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:41.950
projects, including two that will take place
at the same time as the events of The Mandalorian:

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:45.320
An Ahsoka Tano series and Rangers of the New
Republic.

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:49.380
All of these shows will cross over in a big
climactic event, so it's within the realm

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:53.290
of possibility that Din might show up on these
other series as well.

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:57.540
With plenty more adventures coming down the
pipeline, it's likely we're going to see Din

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:01.870
Djarin continue to wrestle with what he has
learned from Bo-Katan Kryze and the Nite Owls.

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:05.520
Other Mandalorians are out there, and they
don't necessarily believe in the same things

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:06.520
he does.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:10.100
Instead, they're out there trying to regain
their homeworld, and the day may come when

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:13.180
the Nite Owls require his assistance for a
certain job.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:17.430
While Din's still not completely comfortable
taking his helmet off, he may get more comfortable

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:21.730
with the idea if he gets the chance to spend
more time with a different sect of Mandalorians

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:24.170
who aren't as strict with the rules as he
is.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:28.740
You can see Pedro Pascal next when the final
episode of The Mandalorian season two airs

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:32.180
on Disney+ on December 18th, 2020.

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:37.350
A week later, on December 25th, you can see
him again over on HBO Max and in theaters

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:40.820
(where they're open) in Wonder Woman 1984.

